# Investigation #05 — Linux SSH Brute Force Detection

## Summary
On 2026-06-22, a brute force attack was detected against the SSH service 
(port 22) on Ubuntu host 192.168.56.102. A total of 143 failed 
authentication attempts were recorded in /var/log/auth.log originating 
from a single source IP within a 60-second window.

## Attack Timeline
| Time | Event |
|------|-------|
| 15:08:01 | First failed SSH login detected |
| 15:08:01–15:08:11 | 143 rapid failed attempts across 4 threads |
| 15:08:11 | Attack stopped (Hydra terminated) |

## IOCs (Indicators of Compromise)
| Type | Value |
|------|-------|
| Attacker IP | 192.168.56.101 |
| Target IP | 192.168.56.102 |
| Target Port | 22 (SSH) |
| Username Targeted | musfira |
| Failed Attempts | 143 |
| Attack Tool | Hydra v9.5 |

## Detection Method
Analyzed /var/log/auth.log using Linux CLI tools:
- grep "Failed password" — extracted failed login events
- awk '{print $13}' — parsed attacker IP from log fields
- wc -l — counted total attempts
- uniq -c | sort -rn — ranked by frequency

## MITRE ATT&CK Mapping
| Tactic | Technique | ID |
|-------------------|--------------------------------|-----------|
| Credential Access | Brute Force: Password Guessing | T1110.001 |
| Initial Access | Exploit Public-Facing Application | T1190 |

## Key Log Evidence
Failed password for musfira from 192.168.56.101 port 22 ssh2
— repeated 143 times within 60 seconds

## Detection Rule (Splunk)
index=linux_logs sourcetype=syslog "Failed password"
| stats count by src_ip, user
| where count > 10
| table src_ip, user, count

## Response Actions
1. Block 192.168.56.101 at firewall
2. Lock account musfira temporarily
3. Enable fail2ban to auto-block after 5 failures
4. Consider moving SSH to non-standard port
5. Enforce SSH key-based authentication, disable password login

## Tools Used
- Hydra v9.5 (attack simulation)
- /var/log/auth.log (detection)
- grep, awk, wc, uniq (log analysis)
